  "facts": [
    {
      "id": "f1",
      "text": "In June 2025 the World Bank raised its international extreme poverty line to $3.00 a person a day in 2021 prices, replacing the $2.15 line based on 2017 prices.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s3"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f2",
      "text": "The line is measured in prices adjusted for purchasing power, so $3.00 stands for the same amount of goods in every country rather than the same sum of local money.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s1",
        "s3"
      ],
      "kind": "definition"
    },
    {
      "id": "f3",
      "text": "Under the new line, about 838 million people lived in extreme poverty in 2022, about 125 million more than the 713 million estimated with the old line.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s3"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f4",
      "text": "Sub-Saharan Africa had the largest upward revision under the new line, 111 million more people, while South Asia had 45 million fewer.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s3"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f5",
      "text": "From 1990 to 2025 the number of people in extreme poverty fell from around 2.3 billion to around 831 million, mainly because of strong economic growth in East Asia and South Asia.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s4"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f6",
      "text": "Poverty reduction has slowed in the last decade, and extreme poverty is increasingly concentrated in Sub-Saharan Africa and in places affected by conflict and fragility.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s4"
      ],
      "kind": "cause_effect"
    },
    {
      "id": "f7",
      "text": "Nearly three-quarters of people in extreme poverty live in rural areas; about one in ten people worldwide lived in extreme poverty in 2025.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s4"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f8",
      "text": "The World Bank's goal is to cut extreme poverty to less than 3 percent of the world's people by 2030, but it projects that about 9 percent will still be extremely poor in 2030.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s3"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f9",
      "text": "In 1990 East Asia and the Pacific had the highest extreme poverty rate of the four groups in the chart, 67.0 percent; by 2020 its rate was 2.7 percent.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s1"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f10",
      "text": "China's extreme poverty rate was 83.1 percent in 1990 and is recorded as 0 from 2019 onward.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s1"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f11",
      "text": "Derived: Sub-Saharan Africa's population grew from about 521 million in 1990 to about 1,168 million in 2020, 2.24 times as many people.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s2"
      ],
      "kind": "number",
      "note": "521,029,972 → 1,168,133,785；1,168,133,785 ÷ 521,029,972 ≈ 2.24"
    },
    {
      "id": "f12",
      "text": "Derived: about 320 million people in Sub-Saharan Africa lived in extreme poverty in 1990 and about 537 million in 2020.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s1",
        "s2"
      ],
      "kind": "number",
      "note": "61.5% × 521,029,972 ≈ 320.4 million；46.0% × 1,168,133,785 ≈ 537.3 million"
    },
    {
      "id": "f13",
      "text": "Derived: about 2,300 million people worldwide were extremely poor in 1990 and about 895 million in 2020.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s1",
        "s2"
      ],
      "kind": "number",
      "note": "43.4% × 5,298,962,018 ≈ 2,299.7 million；11.4% × 7,853,863,139 ≈ 895.3 million"
    },
    {
      "id": "f14",
      "text": "Derived: Sub-Saharan Africa's share of the world's extremely poor people rose from about 14 percent in 1990 to about 60 percent in 2020.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s1",
        "s2"
      ],
      "kind": "number",
      "note": "320.4 ÷ 2,299.7 ≈ 0.139；537.3 ÷ 895.3 ≈ 0.600"
    }
  ],
